#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124286 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/id #uuid "1fd5312f-c992-5218-bcfd-9bc235e9cd53" :question/math-academy-id "e-41" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^3}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/24/2497877ff3bc4a10871103f04502252843e2bdf8a9460c9ae4b32ff5344e921a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = -\\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^3}= \\textrm{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124289 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/id #uuid "08d460c2-32e7-5e20-86cf-4a1c2bf13c28" :question/math-academy-id "e-43" :question/problem "Calculate  $\\lim_\\limits {x\\rightarrow -1} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} .$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y = \\dfrac 1 {x^4}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the left by $1$ unit. This gives $y = \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/99/99f04a25115f084e40e9f8f9b06a6749532a2ba2320ee23d31af050a87957269.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^-)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty, \\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1^+)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4} = \\infty,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to (-1)} \\dfrac{1}{(x+1)^4}= \\infty.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124386 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "ca99dd0a-e2bc-5b6a-8138-ffaac453a0d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-773" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{4x^4}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\dfrac 1 4\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\left(\\dfrac{1}{x^4}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} \\dfrac{1}{x^4} = \\dfrac 1 4\\cdot 0 = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124387 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/id #uuid "96ecee6e-f551-5ecd-9991-0f962190f382" :question/math-academy-id "e-774" :question/problem "Compute $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} \\, .$" :question/worked-solution "For any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to\\pm\\infty}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{P(x)}\\right)  = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $(1-x)^4$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\dfrac{1}{(1-x)^4} = 0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565412 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565323}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc7c654f-b8c1-596d-ae22-8f0756107b34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565323} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f15e0b-05f8-5013-b9b0-8f30387b49a6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565407}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0f04e402-41a0-5006-8eb9-65c2d82f6e13" :question/math-academy-id "q-300983" :question/problem "![](images/64/6469991ee6751110f55ce6462c4c3f2e406d8b61fabcb6c2d2cc1515ec12dd2c.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=$\n\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "From the diagram, ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$\n\nNow, rewrite the target limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since ${x}^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-7}=-2\\cdot 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565447 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cecc548-9538-5c7d-9a8f-bea859b123f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565026} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8c07c0a-9297-5a81-902b-be41db3eb020" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565379}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "940dd7e5-a02e-5ae8-8d31-37203bbfab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-305776" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}(-\\frac{9}{4(x-5)^{6}})\\,=\\,-\\frac{9}{4}\\,\\cdot \\,{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $5$ units. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/57/574b270248f2acc2a2eb3403f5626e420f5f16abc6e9e01dcb147df83e5806b5.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {5}^{-}}\\frac{1}{(x-5)^{6}}=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565218}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b992071f-5ad2-5b15-8cfa-8cae80d5ae5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565218} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3879b6-ecd9-5bd8-a6a5-eca6fd88dfd9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565365}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0883765a-f056-556c-bee6-eafc65b5e91c" :question/math-academy-id "q-334164" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{-3}{{x}^{8}} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[-3\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}] \\\\  & =-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-3\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-3\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565484 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f585e57-5c50-5feb-9150-e466d09dad88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565049} :answer-field/id #uuid "71c427a6-d5aa-56d6-bd23-df19a31d1d11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565362}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc61e8f1-0b37-52d2-8620-d0e767062e04" :question/math-academy-id "q-334180" :question/problem "![](images/d7/d7f1d5de28c6479a2e9b8b33a6da25a4091b71b58f6dc0c535ef390c4cb97799.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $g(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=$\n {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(g(x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }g(x)=-2.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}=-2\\cdot 0=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}}=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565444 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b50ced1a-50ab-57f3-a60d-dd1d9d5bd997" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocals of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565087}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "612ea857-8d13-5f8a-bc55-dd2e8b0ba360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565087} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb2cc812-d3f1-5ddb-9bec-85884d6fb255" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565400}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8006767d-6578-52a2-aa78-8300e8ed36b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-334193" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite our limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{-4}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}\n$$\n\nFor any non-constant polynomial $P(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\pm \\infty }(\\frac{1}{P(x)})=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $({x}^{2}-3)^{3}$ is a non-constant polynomial, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=0.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can evaluate the original limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}-3)^{3}}=-4\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565476 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565130}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d919e2a-d68a-568e-b91e-bde70fca882b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565130} :answer-field/id #uuid "db32905c-5c7a-5bd1-bd14-25355dd7bb61" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3e8207e-9140-5f5f-bfa7-ab83fc0d1420" :question/math-academy-id "q-334233" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{7}{9{x}^{7}}) & =\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{7}{9}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{9}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{7}}=-\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "404bc926-5c60-5349-ad10-84dcc8485d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565032} :answer-field/id #uuid "142b5723-947e-572c-b68d-13e6c9995afd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f421385-b333-57a6-99f9-5c22d85a1c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-334297" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}:$\n\n![](images/ff/ffedd1b83fa2d80b924b0d2f68a3100236347e3222ec171bc60b299579a7af4e.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{6}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565415 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124626 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f015a46e-7493-558d-a1d8-c2d90f9306ab" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Odd Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43429be9-bb0e-5b18-86bb-fbdf79bb94bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565036} {:answer/id #uuid "46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565040} {:answer/id #uuid "70c09658-cf29-5193-8d12-ec120b53f9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565107} {:answer/id #uuid "9849d06c-d6fa-5f44-b558-a71dc686c4f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565167} {:answer/id #uuid "c447fc46-df17-5027-b37f-7ea6e75b40a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565235}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e04dd7-a13a-5fbe-b115-b96df353ce1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565040} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f63655d-d2a8-5b20-b3ce-1835b71b6875" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565368}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1b8c4518-97e5-5ab3-a859-e015cbefbbce" :question/math-academy-id "q-36453" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{7}{3x} & =\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(\\frac{1}{x})=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(\\frac{1}{x})=\\infty .\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{x})=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{x}=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9048a8-fb8e-50a8-9d64-a493cee42eea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564985} {:answer/id #uuid "2b422786-3e36-56eb-94b9-64e09e6899fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565007} {:answer/id #uuid "3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565025} {:answer/id #uuid "459108ee-9fb7-5d34-8d66-9abacde67ea5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565038} {:answer/id #uuid "4b0366d3-18b5-5880-ab15-32e4c49fb81c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565046}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c53219e-92ec-58fb-947a-c883a2d4736a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565025} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71e7b0d-88ab-52d1-8e79-593766af594d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565389}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a8882e4-c45a-5dc1-b53b-97925de4eb41" :question/math-academy-id "q-46970" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\,\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we simplify the limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{9}{12{x}^{4}}={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}}\n$$\n\nThen, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $\\frac{3}{4}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4{x}^{4}} & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}$\n\n![](images/fa/fa92c457186fbdf71552ec79c782af7983e6fa09009e7f0efdd02b8f74e3ab98.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({0}^{+})}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty \n$$\n\nThe positive factor of $\\frac{3}{4}$ does not change the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a39ec0dc-20a4-50a6-871b-a8bc6f99aaa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123561} {:answer/id #uuid "3d8b15b5-04e7-56f5-889a-8191d462785d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123564} {:answer/id #uuid "3d34d34c-9884-5f4e-af4d-aaa1afcf0af4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565027} {:answer/id #uuid "e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565282} {:answer/id #uuid "fa250561-2f81-526b-9bb1-37d1f2aa9f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565320}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e74e9bae-3887-560b-b3fc-c5b404b72006" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565282} :answer-field/id #uuid "8745453d-9410-5078-9c89-dc59dd94a1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186123560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5471135-7221-5e32-8634-6bbbec676d01" :question/math-academy-id "q-46972" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can sketch the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}$ as follows:\n\n1\\. Take the graph of $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.$\n\n2\\. Translate it to the right by $1$ unit. This gives $y=\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e4a6bad4e26a2f078930ca95d56999b315cfe55328367bbb1508333891a10618.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{-})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to ({1}^{+})}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty ,\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{1}{(x-1)^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124573 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1d13b888-0ef7-5194-a54d-435d9b344749" :knowledge-point/title "Limits at Infinity of Reciprocal Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1758365f-a72d-56cb-8910-850f490ea058" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564975} {:answer/id #uuid "55a68899-fd6b-5e51-8741-e8646384dc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565064} {:answer/id #uuid "6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565106} {:answer/id #uuid "a65e4a14-81ca-54eb-9501-b356ec107bdf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565191} {:answer/id #uuid "d7f23a02-6b5e-5b17-bde8-5879ecbee74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6feaeecb-9e4c-51ac-9b20-5f7954edd8b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565106} :answer-field/id #uuid "a3143b2c-7af2-5445-aaba-827a5cd2b88e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fdb51987-aeea-5d6d-9fcd-a012fef3d69f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78081" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $-7:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{7}{{x}^{5}}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-7\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}) \\\\  & =-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=0,\n$$\n\nwe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n-7\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{{x}^{5}}=-7\\cdot 0=0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186565409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124627 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "038fb60a-e596-5e48-b05e-f8414d454518" :knowledge-point/title "Infinite Limits of Reciprocal Functions Where the Leading Term Has an Even Power"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f7ee46e-dc63-58c3-89bb-875458d30291" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186564964} {:answer/id #uuid "360b1df4-8a51-5c90-827b-ce21bb471dc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565018} {:answer/id #uuid "500e4e0d-01de-5798-a08b-46e3ec45ecf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565051} {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565078} {:answer/id #uuid "63379f06-acca-58df-bcc7-05308d492f18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565091}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c3a2024-7096-5374-881d-7381cde4fee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565078} :answer-field/id #uuid "b673d111-8088-5a19-9579-504b507d8a88" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186565377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "0443135d-0cff-565f-8ee8-219a9e567bef" :question/math-academy-id "q-99351" :question/problem "Compute ${lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}(-\\frac{9}{5{x}^{8}})=-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}\n$$\n\nNow, we recall the graph of the function $y=\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}:$\n\n![](images/ae/aeeed013b57f5fd8a554ca6613bf9e3fa9419ae2e0fd5920397c5b9cb0dd88fb.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nFinally, the negative factor of $-\\frac{9}{5}$ changes the sign of the limit. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n-\\frac{9}{5}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\frac{1}{{x}^{8}}=-\\infty .\n$$"}]}
